'''Corporació Petrolífera de l'Estat de Balboa''' (CPEB) is a {{w|state-owned enterprise|state-owned}} petroleum company of [[Balboa]]. It manages the exploration, extraction, and production of domestic offshore oil resources in Balboan territorial waters. The company produces approximately 185,000 barrels per day, the majority of which is supplied to the domestic market for refining and consumption.

CPEB is distinct from [[CIEA]], the large integrated supermajor that dominates Balboa's petroleum sector, in that CPEB's mandate is limited to domestic upstream operations — the extraction of Balboa's own offshore oil — rather than international exploration, refining, or distribution. CPEB's crude is processed primarily at [[CIEA]]'s [[Badia Curta]] refinery under supply agreements.

== History ==

=== Discovery of offshore oil ===

Prior to the 1980s, Balboa was not known to possess any domestic oil reserves. The country had no history of petroleum exploration, and the Badia Curta refinery — operated by [[CIEA]] (then Petrolí de Balboa) — relied entirely on imported crude. The discovery of oil in Balboan waters was accidental.

Heavy oil extraction was already underway in the [[Illes dels Pescadors]] and southern [[Sanrobonia]], both nearby in the Shendan. In the late 1970s and early 1980s, international oil companies conducting exploratory drilling in the deepwater Shendan Sea selected test sites that they believed lay within the Illes dels Pescadors' exclusive economic zone. Subsequent surveys revealed that several of the test sites were in fact located within Balboa's EEZ, in deep waters off the [[Tramuntana]] coast near the outer limit of Balboa's maritime boundary.

The discovery was modest by global standards — the reservoirs were smaller and deeper than those being exploited in the Illes dels Pescadors and Sanrobonia — but it was significant for Balboa, which had previously been considered to have no domestic petroleum resources at all.

The Balboan government, under the BPP administration, opted to establish a state-owned entity to manage the extraction of the domestic offshore reserves rather than privatising the fields or licensing them to foreign operators. The decision reflected the government's broader strategy of maintaining public control over strategic natural resources.

=== Formation (1984) ===

CPEB was established in 1984 as a state-owned enterprise under the Ministry of Energy and Natural Resources. The company was given exclusive rights to explore and produce oil within Balboan territorial waters, and was tasked with supplying the domestic market to reduce Balboa's dependence on imported crude.

The company began production from the first offshore platform in 1987, with initial output of approximately 40,000 barrels per day. Production grew steadily through the 1990s and 2000s as additional fields were brought online.

=== Development ===

Through the 1990s and 2000s, CPEB expanded its offshore production infrastructure, adding new platforms and subsea pipelines. The company also invested in enhanced oil recovery techniques to maintain output from maturing fields. By the mid-2020s, production had reached approximately 185,000 barrels per day.

Despite the growth in domestic production, Balboa remains a net importer of crude oil, as domestic consumption — driven by the Badia Curta refinery and the broader petrochemical chain — exceeds CPEB's output. The company's production covers a portion of domestic needs, with the balance supplied by CIEA's international imports.

== Operations ==

* '''Headquarters''': [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]]
* '''Production''': approximately 185,000 barrels per day (2026)
* '''Fields''': Deepwater offshore platforms in the Shendan Sea, in deep waters off the [[Tramuntana]] coast near the outer limit of Balboa's [[exclusive economic zone]]
* '''Employees''': approximately 2,800 (2026)

CPEB operates a number of deepwater offshore production platforms in the Shendan Sea, in deep waters off the Tramuntana coast, near the outer limit of Balboa's exclusive economic zone. The fields are connected by subsea pipelines to onshore receiving terminals on the Tramuntana coast, from which the crude is transported to the [[Badia Curta]] refinery for processing by [[CIEA]] under long-term supply agreements.

The company does not operate its own refining, distribution, or retail operations. Its role is strictly upstream — exploration, drilling, production, and delivery of crude to the domestic refining system.

== Relationship with CIEA ==

CPEB and [[CIEA]] operate in complementary but distinct roles within Balboa's petroleum sector. CIEA, as the dominant integrated oil company, imports the majority of Balboa's crude from its international fields in [[Sanrobonia]] and [[Potocsí]], refines it at Badia Curta, and manages the downstream petrochemical chain. CPEB, by contrast, extracts only domestic offshore oil and supplies it to the same refinery.

The supply agreements between CPEB and CIEA ensure that domestic crude is processed at Badia Curta alongside imported crude, reducing the volume of imports required to meet domestic consumption. The arrangement is regulated by the Ministry of Energy and Natural Resources, which sets the pricing terms for CPEB's crude deliveries to the refinery.

== Ownership ==

CPEB is wholly owned by the [[Government of Balboa]]. It operates under the oversight of the Ministry of Energy and Natural Resources and is not listed on any stock exchange. The company's revenues accrue to the state budget, with a portion reinvested in exploration and production infrastructure.

